+# Packet FIX-131 — a redelivered message's timeline is stitched from two runs
+
+Module: `flottila-core` (test only). Row: `agent/BACKLOG.md`, FIX-131.
+
+## Scope, and what this task is NOT
+
+This task does **not** fix the defect. The fix is keying — `(id, attempt, step)` — and belongs to
+CONSIDER-12 under D-107/D-108. This task produces the test that pins today's behaviour exactly, so
+that CONSIDER-12 has a regression test to invert and so the defect cannot be rediscovered a third
+time.
+
+The row said "confirm against a real Postgres under `-Pit` before designing anything". **That
+premise is withdrawn by the architect**: recon established that both store implementations drop the
+duplicate by construction, so a real Postgres adds nothing to the confirmation. No Docker, no
+`-Pit`, no Testcontainers in this task.
+
+## Facts recon established — do not re-derive them, do rely on them
+
+- `PgMessageStore.java:51-55` inserts into `messages(...)` with `on conflict (id, step) do nothing`.
+  `messages` has no attempt column; the attempt columns in `deploy/postgres` are on `work`
+  (`V4__work_attempts.sql:4`). Highest migration on disk is `V11__drop_payloads.sql`.
+- `InMemoryMessageStore.record` does `rows.putIfAbsent(new Row(snapshot.messageId(),
+  snapshot.step()), snapshot)` (`flottila-core/src/main/java/io/flottila/core/record/InMemoryMessageStore.java`,
+  the `record` method). The same drop as Postgres, which is why this is a core test.
+- `Snapshot` (`Snapshot.java:36-38`) has no attempt component. `Envelope.ATTEMPT` is a props key
+  (`Envelope.java:40`); `FlowWorker.java:338` sets it on rerun ingress when `item.attempts() > 1`;
+  nothing in the `record` package reads it. So two runs of one id write the same row keys.
+- `MessageStore.timeline(String)` (`MessageStore.java:30`) returns the rows in step order — Postgres
+  `where id = $1 order by step` (`:58-59`), in-memory sorts by step.
+- `PgMessageStoreIT:122-132` already pins the Postgres half, under the name
+  `aBatchCarriesManyMessagesAndDuplicatesAreIgnored`: it records `m-7` step 1 as `to`, flushes,
+  records `to-again` for the same `(id, step)`, and asserts the step-1 name is still `to`.
+- No existing test runs one message id through a pipeline twice and asserts the timeline.
+  `FlowWorkerTest.aLeaseNobodyRenewsExpiresAfterOneLease:360` makes a second claim with attempts 2
+  (`:385-388`) but asserts no timeline rows; `crashFirst` (`:191-193`) reruns with `ATTEMPT` set.
+  `FlowWorkerTest` uses the in-memory store (class javadoc, `:76`).
+
+## The harm, stated precisely — this is what the test must show
+
+The row says the message "loses the history of every run but its first". That undersells it. If run
+1 fails at step 3 and run 2 reaches step 7, `timeline(id)` returns **steps 1-3 from run 1 and steps
+4-7 from run 2, in one sequence, with nothing marking the seam** — and step 3 reads `FAILED` while
+steps 4-7 read `OK`, which is a sequence no single run can produce. The timeline is not short, it is
+a chimera, and an operator reading it cannot tell what happened. That is the observation worth
+pinning.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+1. One test in `flottila-core` drives **one** message id through `FlowWorker` twice against the
+   in-memory store: run 1 fails at a middle step, the lease expires or a second claim happens, run 2
+   gets past that step. Reuse the machinery `FlowWorkerTest` already has rather than inventing a
+   path.
+2. `timeline(id)` is then asserted **row by row**: which step numbers exist, and for each row which
+   run it came from — distinguished by a value the two runs genuinely differ in (step name, outcome,
+   or a prop), not by position. Not a size assertion, not a `contains`.
+3. The stitch is asserted explicitly: a `FAILED` row at step N and `OK` rows at steps greater than N
+   in the same timeline.
+4. Whether the attempt is visible anywhere in that timeline is asserted either way. If
+   `Envelope.ATTEMPT` does reach run 2's props and so appears in the props of the rows run 2 did
+   write, assert that it does and that it is absent from the rows run 1 wrote — that asymmetry is
+   the one place a reader could notice two runs, and CONSIDER-12 needs to know it exists. If it
+   reaches nothing, assert its absence from every row.
+5. The test is **green on today's code**, and its naming makes unmistakable that it documents a
+   defect: the method name states the wrong behaviour as a fact, and the javadoc cites FIX-131, F-29
+   and CONSIDER-12 and says the test is to be inverted when the row key gains `attempt`. Do not give
+   it a name that reads as intended behaviour — `...DuplicatesAreIgnored` at `PgMessageStoreIT:122`
+   is exactly that trap, and it is part of why this went unnoticed.
+6. No production change, and no new seam added to production code for testability.
+
+## Red first — deliberately absent, and the one thing that outranks the test
+
+There is no red here: the output is a true description of today's behaviour. **If what you observe
+is not what the section above describes** — the second run never writes colliding rows, the worker
+does not re-run ingress recording, the message is re-identified somewhere, or the timeline comes
+back some third way — then **stop, write no test, and hand back exactly what you observed with the
+file and line that caused it**. A correction to the architect's description is worth more than the
+test, and you will not be marked down for it.
+
+## Allowed paths
+
+- `flottila-core/src/test/java/io/flottila/core/flow/` — a new test class, or an addition to
+  `FlowWorkerTest`. Prefer a new class if the setup is long.
+
+Nothing else. In particular do **not** touch any `BatchingWriter*` file in this module: FIX-71 is in
+flight on those files in another tree, and a change here would collide with it.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+The fix itself. `(id, attempt, step)`, a migration, any change to `Snapshot`, `PgMessageStore`,
+`InMemoryMessageStore` or `FlowWorker`. FIX-128 and FIX-129. Do not "tidy" `FlowWorkerTest`.
