+# Packet FIX-195 (class B) — BatchingWriter: refused windows commit, Errors fail only their own item
+
+Module: `flottila-core`. Row: `agent/BACKLOG.md`, FIX-195, written 2026-11-15 after the merge of
+the core rework into the session branch.
+
+## Read this first, or you will attribute the policy to the wrong decision
+
+This task re-applies a policy the owner approved on 2026-11-14, which reached `main` nowhere. On
+the session branch it was recorded as **D-104**. On `main`, **D-104 is a different decision** ("No
+measurement of the old core before the rework"). Do not cite D-104. The policy is re-recorded in
+this task under **D-144**, and D-144 is what the code and the tests cite.
+
+Attributing a rule to a decision that does not contain it is the defect class recorded as F-27 on
+the session branch. Do not create it here.
+
+## The defect
+
+`BatchingWriter` was rewritten by the core rework from a base that predates FIX-71, so two things
+the owner decided are absent from `main`:
+
+**(a) Two of the three catch clauses are narrow again.** `BatchingWriter.java:173` and `:309`
+catch `RuntimeException`; `:228` catches `RuntimeException | Error`. On the session branch all
+four sites caught `RuntimeException | Error`. Observed consequence, from the branch's own tests
+run against `main` (`agent/work/merge-verify-full.log`):
+
+- `InternalError "sink cannot write"` escapes instead of failing its batch
+- `IllegalStateException "cancel refused"` is thrown at the flusher
+- a committed item's completion is lost — the test saw `Future{unresolved}`
+
+**(b) A refused window still fails its items.** `main` keeps `Refused` (`:70`),
+`Drained.refused` (`:73`) and the refused branch of dispatch (`:280`) — the shape the policy
+removes. There is no `windowsRefused()` counter: that symbol is why
+`BatchingWriterWindowRefusalLogTest` would not compile at the merge.
+
+Both test classes that pinned this were taken out of the tree at the merge (commit after
+`5b5946d`), so nothing pins any of it today.
+
+Why it is not deferred with the other FIX rows: `BatchingWriter` is the monitoring write path, and
+CORE-GATE measures that path. Measuring it with an escaping `Error` measures something other than
+what ships.
+
+## The policy to re-record as D-144
+
+When the window `timer.schedule` throws — `RuntimeException` or `Error` — the batches the drain
+just took are **committed, never failed**. They stay in flight, reach the sink in the order taken,
+and whatever stayed queued rides their completions. Nothing stays queued with no commit in flight
+and no window, and no item is failed by the refusal.
+
+What it replaces: one refusal failing up to `maxBacklog` rows plus every waiter, never retried.
+What is sacrificed: tempo, and the newest rows under sustained load. That trade is one-sided for
+trail rows, which is why the owner took it.
+
+Observability: a counter that always rises, and a WARNING on **the first refusal of an episode and
+on recovery** — not once per refusal. Two lines per episode, so a permanently refusing timer
+cannot flood the log and silence stays impossible.
+
+The rule to pin: **no public method of `BatchingWriter` ever completes by throwing; every throw
+becomes the item's failure cause.** Not swallowed — it is logged with its cause.
+
+## Acceptance — the tests this task ends with
+
+Red first, each observed red before the code changes. Adapt each to the reworked class; the names
+are the branch's and may be kept.
+
+1. `aRefusedWindowCommitsTheTakenBatchAndEveryItemSucceeds`
+2. `aPermanentlyRefusedWindowDrainsTheWholeBacklogAndEveryWaiter` — `backlog() == 0`,
+   `waiting() == 0`, no item failed with the timer's cause
+3. `anErrorFromTheWindowScheduleIsCommittedAndTheWriterStaysUsable`
+4. `aRefusedWindowIsCountedAndNotSilent` — the counter rises once per refusal; the WARNING appears
+   on the first refusal of an episode and on recovery only
+5. `anErrorFromAWaiterDeadlineScheduleFailsOnlyThatSaveWithoutThrowing` — `assertDoesNotThrow`, a
+   failed future carrying that `Error`, `waiting() == 0`, the writer still usable
+6. `anErrorFromTheSinkFailsOnlyItsBatchAndReleasesItsSlot` — the items fail, `inFlight() == 0`,
+   the writer still usable
+7. `oneDrainHandsItsBatchesToTheSinkOldestFirst` — with a sink that records the lists it is handed
+   and completes only on demand; with `commitConcurrency 2` and two full batches,
